From 8ed8399f042b3b3265ad941e0a971da492c8667c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Serhii Nikolaichuk <nikolaichuk.s.f@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 15:52:02 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] sevsnp: refuse a debuggable guest, and check the intermediate
 against AMD's CRL

POLICY stays out of the reference-value matching, but a guest the
hypervisor may debug (DEBUG) or one bound to a migration agent (MIGRATE_MA),
or a POLICY with its reserved bits wrong, is no longer affirmed: the
configuration claim becomes unsupportable.

After the report's signature and chain, the chain's intermediate (the ASK,
or the ASVK for a VLEK) is checked against the CRL at its distribution
point on AMD's KDS: signed by the ARK already compared with the trust
anchor, current, and the intermediate not listed. Each CRL is fetched once
per validity period, so the KDS is not asked per report; the check fails
closed. Each check that passes is logged at INFO with the intermediate
and the dates of the CRL it was checked against, so the log of every
appraisal shows it. Tests use AMD's CRLs of 30 September (test/crl/)
and never reach the network.

Ported onto the chain check up to AMD's roots (go-sev-guest v0.15.0):
the intermediate checked against the CRL is the one among those roots
that signed the report's certificate, with the ARK that certifies it,
not the ASK and ARK the evidence carries. The KDS serves the ARK's one
CRL at the VLEK's distribution point as well, byte for byte, so the
tests serve the same snapshot for both.
